  "live_records": {
    "q-149437": {
      "dom_id": "step-q149437",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[0,12].$ \nUsing a right Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=12$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=12$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{12-0}{3}=4.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *right* Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈\\pi \\cdot (g(4)+g(8)+g(12))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}$ | $1$ | $18$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\pi \\cdot (g(4)+g(8)+g(12))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot (18+0+8)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 26\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =104\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
