    "q-149498": {
      "dom_id": "step-q149498",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x\\in [5,10],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (5,10).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[5,10]$ using a right Riemann sum. Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)<0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)<0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum overestimates the true area.",
      "worked_solution": "We're given that $g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Differentiating $g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(5,10),$ we have \n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{x}^{3}>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{3{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{3}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{3{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
