    "q-346317": {
      "dom_id": "step-q346317",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The arc length of a smooth curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ over the closed interval $[-4,2].$\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $\\sqrt{63}$ | $\\sqrt{15}$ | $\\sqrt{8}$ | $\\sqrt{48}$ |\n\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length $L$ of the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-4$ and $x=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Using the hint, we have that the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-4$ and $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{-4}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-4)}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈(g(-4)+g(-2)+g(0))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nNow, we set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $\\sqrt{63}$ | $\\sqrt{15}$ | $\\sqrt{8}$ | $\\sqrt{48}$ |\n| $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}$ | $8$ | $4$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our arc length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈(g(-4)+g(-2)+g(0))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(8+4+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
